Steps to configure SSH for git

1. Generate Nw SSH Key
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "vasu.seelamneni@gmail.com"
If your system doesn’t support ED25519:
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -C "vasu.seelamneni@gmail.com"

When prompted:
File location: press Enter
(default: ~/.ssh/id_ed25519)

Passphrase: optional (press Enter for none)

This creates two files:

File	Purpose
id_ed25519	Private key (keep safe, never share)
id_ed25519.pub	Public key (upload to GitHub)

2. Start the SSH Agent
Windows (PowerShell):
eval $(ssh-agent -s)
ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

Linux/macOS:
eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"
ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

3. Add SSH Key to GitHub
    Log in to GitHub
    Go to Settings
    Select SSH and GPG keys
    Click New SSH key
    Copy your public key:
cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
    Paste it into GitHub → Save.

4. Test SSH Connection
ssh -T git@github.com
Hi vasu! You've successfully authenticated, but GitHub does not provide shell access.

5. Configure Git to Use SSH
    Check current remote:
    git remote -v

    If you see:
    https://github.com/username/repo.git
    Change it to SSH:

    git remote set-url origin git@github.com:username/repo.git